WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.537
Let's walk through version one point two point one of the Exchange Korean
WordBreaker workaround.

00:00:06.757 --> 00:00:13.772
We'll use one script for local checks, remote servers, and a CSV roster, then make
sense of the results.

00:00:13.992 --> 00:00:19.695
This is custom automation, not a Microsoft-signed hotfix or a permanent product fix.

00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:25.246
The commands and output are illustrative. This video does not perform a new
deployment.

00:00:25.466 --> 00:00:32.178
The safe sequence remains: verify, detect, preview, pilot, validate, and only then
expand.

00:00:33.397 --> 00:00:38.023
GitHub distributes source and tests, not the Microsoft rule binaries.

00:00:38.243 --> 00:00:43.723
Build the deployment package on an elevated management workstation, never on
Exchange.

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:51.139
This example uses rule files you already have. The builder still verifies their
exact sizes and hashes.

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:58.386
Alternatively, supply the approved Microsoft media, or explicitly choose the
documented download option.

00:00:58.606 --> 00:01:04.377
The result is a deployment zip and checksum sidecar. SQL Server is not installed.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:10.695
Review the code, licensing, signing policy, and current Microsoft guidance before
proceeding.

00:01:11.897 --> 00:01:16.885
Compare the archive hash with your trusted build record and stop on any mismatch.

00:01:17.105 --> 00:01:23.781
The guide contains the complete guard. A sidecar from an untrusted download is not
authentication.

00:01:24.001 --> 00:01:29.461
Verify a fresh extraction, then keep the reviewed runtime in a stable working
folder.

00:01:29.681 --> 00:01:33.286
For example, use the unversioned Scripts folder shown here.

00:01:33.506 --> 00:01:39.637
When updating, preserve user edits and reports, and do not replace code while a run
is active.

00:01:40.855 --> 00:01:44.736
Run the primary script without a mode to detect the current state.

00:01:44.956 --> 00:01:53.305
Local interactive runs can request normal Windows UAC elevation into sixty-four-bit
PowerShell five point one.

00:01:53.525 --> 00:01:59.357
Automation, remoting, and local JSON capture must already have the required rights.

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:04.587
Detect shows one Status column, followed by the action taken and next step.

00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:09.861
Here the identity matches and both files are absent. Nothing has been applied.

00:02:10.081 --> 00:02:16.040
Detect does not modify Exchange files or services, though a normal run saves
reports.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:22.658
On an ineligible installation, No, stop is red, and the missing files are yellow.

00:02:22.878 --> 00:02:25.971
Do not change the manifest to make that server pass.

00:02:26.191 --> 00:02:31.811
A confirmed identity match is green. Missing is green only with a matching identity.

00:02:32.031 --> 00:02:35.455
Present is also green, but means existence only.

00:02:35.675 --> 00:02:42.137
If either rule exists, the tool stops. Even matching file hashes are not permission
to reapply.

00:02:42.357 --> 00:02:47.337
Read the overall status and next step. Color alone never proves recovery.

00:02:48.522 --> 00:02:56.072
For remote inventory, add Computer Name. For a larger environment, supply a reviewed
CSV roster instead.

00:02:56.292 --> 00:03:01.825
The Computer Name header is required, and every target is validated before any
connection.

00:03:02.045 --> 00:03:08.005
Blank or duplicate targets and malformed rows stop the run. Input order is
preserved.

00:03:08.225 --> 00:03:13.320
Extra columns are metadata only. They do not skip targets or grant approval.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:18.614
Use existing administrative Kerberos access to the normal PowerShell endpoint.

00:03:18.834 --> 00:03:25.104
No report directory is required, and no trust settings or credentials are changed
automatically.

00:03:26.313 --> 00:03:32.423
After the command, dollar report contains real PowerShell objects, one row per
target.

00:03:32.643 --> 00:03:37.397
The terminal prints a summary, and dollar report Files holds the export paths.

00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:42.948
Filter or format the objects without parsing JSON or scraping the console.

00:03:43.168 --> 00:03:46.719
Use Pass Through when you want explicit pipeline assignment.

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:52.237
A successful automatic UAC handoff returns the data to the original session.

00:03:52.457 --> 00:03:58.022
An unrelated external PowerShell process cannot set variables in your existing
shell.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.772
CSV is the easiest format for a spreadsheet: one server per row.

00:04:03.992 --> 00:04:10.538
JSON preserves field names, booleans, arrays, and nested detail for another script
or system.

00:04:10.758 --> 00:04:17.837
Normal local and remote runs export CSV, detailed JSON, and JSON Lines by default.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:23.133
Each run gets a unique folder under the default report location on the caller.

00:04:23.353 --> 00:04:29.206
Report Directory overrides the root, and No CSV omits only the CSV file.

00:04:29.426 --> 00:04:36.441
What If keeps results in memory. An invalid path or export failure is an error, not
a silent fallback.

00:04:37.647 --> 00:04:42.071
For a Splunk file collector, use the finalized JSON Lines file.

00:04:42.291 --> 00:04:49.125
It has one complete event per line, with a run ID, target name, and UTC finalization
time.

00:04:49.345 --> 00:04:54.669
Do not tail the detailed rollout file as final events, because checkpoints rewrite
it.

00:04:54.889 --> 00:05:00.006
The guide includes event boundaries, timestamps, and field extraction examples.

00:05:00.226 --> 00:05:07.317
Do not combine search-time JSON extraction with indexed JSON extraction for the same
source type.

00:05:07.537 --> 00:05:14.607
The customer's Splunk administrator must validate ingestion. No live Splunk
integration is being claimed.

00:05:15.813 --> 00:05:19.107
On a still-eligible target, run Apply with What If.

00:05:19.327 --> 00:05:25.009
This checks local applicability and payload without copying files or restarting
Search.

00:05:25.229 --> 00:05:29.642
Only Apply, including its preview, gets Before and Current columns.

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:33.797
They describe this invocation, not reconstructed history.

00:05:34.017 --> 00:05:38.994
A preview can still fail on an ineligible installation or existing rules.

00:05:39.214 --> 00:05:45.525
Remote What If is different: it validates the roster and shows a plan, without
contacting targets.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:51.165
After the actual maintenance and health checks, approve one pilot server.

00:05:51.385 --> 00:05:57.132
The primary script stages files only unless Restart Search is explicitly supplied.

00:05:57.352 --> 00:06:03.419
With the approved window and restart requested, it adds the two files without
overwriting anything.

00:06:03.639 --> 00:06:11.049
It verifies copied identities and normal inherited read permissions, then restarts
only Host Controller Service.

00:06:11.269 --> 00:06:17.347
The tool does not drain transport, move databases, or place Exchange into
maintenance mode.

00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:24.423
Without the restart switch, exit ten means files changed and a controlled Search
restart is still required.

00:06:25.605 --> 00:06:33.475
The restart path observes the exact Content Engine process and the same process ID
for thirty seconds by default.

00:06:33.695 --> 00:06:37.587
That is a bounded startup check, not workload certification.

00:06:37.807 --> 00:06:41.935
Use a mailbox whose active database is on the changed server.

00:06:42.155 --> 00:06:48.190
Test new ordinary and Korean delivery and server-side search, including a negative
control.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:53.763
Check the original symptoms and fresh errors, and assess historical backlog
separately.

00:06:53.983 --> 00:06:58.322
Do not proceed to another server until the required evidence passes.

00:06:59.522 --> 00:07:04.853
For expansion, use the primary script with approved names or a reviewed CSV.

00:07:05.073 --> 00:07:10.490
A restarted remote rollout is serial and requires a local interactive console.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:16.702
After each server, perform the workload checks before entering the exact recovery
attestation.

00:07:16.922 --> 00:07:20.376
There is no unattended or parallel restart shortcut.

00:07:20.596 --> 00:07:26.407
The old Fleet script remains only for compatibility, and its Apply still includes a
restart.

00:07:26.627 --> 00:07:33.375
The primary script calls the shared module directly, not that wrapper. Use the
primary for new procedures.

00:07:34.563 --> 00:07:38.602
Always interpret the status, action, and exit code together.

00:07:38.822 --> 00:07:47.012
Zero is not proof of recovery. One is an error. Ten is a pending Search restart, not
a Windows reboot request.

00:07:47.232 --> 00:07:51.891
Twenty means an applicability or existing-rule stop that needs review.

00:07:52.111 --> 00:07:59.447
Modifying operations keep protected receipts and events. A partial failure is not
automatically undone.

00:07:59.667 --> 00:08:04.667
Do not blindly retry Apply, and do not invent Support approval for rollback.

00:08:04.887 --> 00:08:11.648
Deployment-agent command wrappers must explicitly forward dollar last exit code, as
shown in the guide.

00:08:12.855 --> 00:08:18.324
If files look right but the workload still fails, stop rollout and preserve
evidence.

00:08:18.544 --> 00:08:24.025
The earlier lab pilot found stale caller connections after the Content Engine
restart.

00:08:24.245 --> 00:08:31.272
An event provider name did not identify the originating service; process IDs and
feeder logs did.

00:08:31.492 --> 00:08:36.022
Any targeted caller recovery required separate evidence and approval.

00:08:36.242 --> 00:08:44.111
Later English and Korean EWS checks passed, but that did not certify Outlook, OWA,
or historical backlog.

00:08:44.331 --> 00:08:49.554
Do not turn that lab-specific recovery into a blanket restart recommendation.

00:08:50.772 --> 00:08:58.834
Version one point two point one passed one hundred seventy-seven offline tests,
including the downloadable source archive.

00:08:59.054 --> 00:09:07.382
Read-only lab detection also verified the staged package. No new Apply or restart
was performed for the color update.

00:09:07.602 --> 00:09:12.708
These checks support the tool's behavior, not a claim that your workload is
recovered.

00:09:12.928 --> 00:09:17.907
Use the current guide, the reporting and Splunk notes, and the validation summary.

00:09:18.127 --> 00:09:25.357
Verify the package, pilot carefully, retain your evidence, and expand only after the
workload checks pass.
